1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,835
Still writing in Markdown, but the results feel too plain?

2
00:00:03,785 --> 00:00:08,833
Want rich HTML results, but writing HTML feels too complex?

3
00:00:08,833 --> 00:00:11,595
MDS is here to clear that friction.

4
00:00:11,595 --> 00:00:14,952
Keep writing Markdown — get web-grade results.

5
00:00:14,952 --> 00:00:18,666
Write Markdown as usual, add a few semantic blocks.

6
00:00:18,666 --> 00:00:26,404
Layout, motion, and interaction go to the MDS renderer, and you get rich, polished pages that feel like HTML.

7
00:00:26,404 --> 00:00:28,738
Let's look at the authoring flow.

8
00:00:28,738 --> 00:00:30,880
Start with plain Markdown.

9
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:36,678
Headings, lists, quotes — the MDS renderer turns them into beautiful HTML.

10
00:00:36,678 --> 00:00:38,500
Then write a hero.

11
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:41,880
A title, one line of copy, and a button.

12
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:45,666
The MDS renderer turns it into a real page right away.

13
00:00:45,666 --> 00:00:50,035
Of course, you can also write controls the way you would in HTML.

14
00:00:50,035 --> 00:00:55,023
Buttons, sliders, switches — the MDS renderer renders those too.

15
00:00:55,023 --> 00:00:57,857
There are several default themes to choose from.

16
00:00:57,857 --> 00:01:03,130
default, light, dark, and rich — or build your own if you want something different.

17
00:01:03,130 --> 00:01:05,261
Public Beta is live.

18
00:01:05,261 --> 00:01:07,976
Use the commands below to get started.

19
00:01:07,976 --> 00:01:11,238
The source is on GitHub — feedback is welcome.

